    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 110526\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1026 PM MST Thu Sep 10 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures will prevail through the rest of the\n  week, with Extreme Heat Warnings in effect for portions of the\n  lower deserts.\n\n- Daily chances for scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms\n  will continue to be focused over the Eastern Arizona high\n  terrain. Any chances for lower deserts should be confined to\n  late Friday and again Sunday into Monday.\n\n- Cooling temperatures over the weekend into early next week\n  should eventually push readings back into the normal range by\n  around next Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nThe high remains centered over southwestern AZ and southeastern \nCA with generally easterly flow aloft over the region. Mid to high\nlevel moisture has stayed put with 1000-700 mb mixing ratios \nstill ranging between 10-12g/kg across the lower deserts and into \nthe eastern high terrain. While we have plenty of moisture (PWATs \n140-160% of normal), with a strong lower level subsidence \ninversion in place convection will struggle to have any \ndevelopment for most areas, especially after SE AZ had extensive \nactivity last night, which may have overworked the atmosphere too \nmuch for anything developing tonight. This is reflected in the \nlatest CAMs and HREF showing <10% chance of activity in the far \neastern high terrain, but near a 30% chance of light showers \nlater this afternoon in western Imperial County, with convection  \nalready visible on satellite developing in Northern Baja.\n\nThe peak strength of the ridge occurred late yesterday, translating \nto peak temperatures today with the highs between 106F-110F in SC AZ \nand 108F-112F in the western lower deserts and along the Colorado \nRiver Valley. Additionally, the lower deserts of southeast \nCalifornia are expected to feel especially unbearable as heat indices \nwill be up to around 115 degrees today. With above normal \ntemperatures and more humid conditions be sure to take extra care \nwhen spending time outdoors. The Extreme Heat Warning remains in \neffect through this evening for SC AZ, and the Phoenix Metro, and \nthrough Friday evening along the Colorado River Valley, Yuma area, \nand southeastern Imperial Valley. The upper level ridge will continue \nto weaken today into Friday, likely weakening enough to lower \ntemperatures a good 2-4 degrees across south-central Arizona \nstarting Friday and 1-3 degrees over the western deserts.\n\nFriday should also bring another influx of moisture from the south \nsouthwest, pushing area low level mixing ratios more into a 11-12 \ng/kg range. However, this may still not be enough to spark off more \nwidespread convection as the the subsidence inversion will still be \nin place (albeit slightly weaker) and the steering flow is expected \nto turn out of the southwest. We are expecting better storm coverage \nacross the eastern Arizona high terrain on Friday, but with a \nsouthwesterly steering flow it will be hard to get convection to \nfire over the lower deserts. The latest NBM run continued to show \nPoPs between 25-25% over SC AZ and eastwards, however with the \natmospheric conditions expected to be in place PoPs have been \nreduced to 5-15% with the higher range being mostly confined to\nthe east of Phoenix. If storms do actually get going over the \nsouth- central Arizona lower deserts Friday evening, they will be \nwithin a high DCAPE environment which means strong winds will be a\npossibility. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nForecast confidence for the weekend into next week remains quite\nlow, especially when looking at moisture levels and rain chances.\nGuidance does agree the subtropical ridge will get pushed to the\neast of our region over the weekend as a Pacific trough moves\nthrough the northern tier states, but the GEFS is stronger and\nfarther south with the trough compared to the EPS. The models also\ndiffer in the amount of moisture and rain chances during the\nperiod with the GEFS favoring better rain chances from Phoenix and\nareas east of Phoenix over the weekend, while the EPS favors\nbetter chances by Tuesday into the middle of next week. Needless\nto say, these differences do not inspire much confidence in the\navailable NBM/WPC PoPs. \n\nThe latest forecast places little if any PoPs on Saturday and \nSaturday night, which seems fairly reasonable, but then seems to \nbe under doing chances late Sunday. The increased PoPs of 20-40% \nthen seem too high for Monday before gradually lowering during the\nmiddle part of next week. Lately, guidance has had a tendency to \nplace fairly high PoPs during the 4-8 day range, just to back off \nas it gets into the short-term. It would seem everything would \nhave to come together nearly perfectly with the trough passing by \nto our north close enough, but not too close to scour out what \nmoisture we have in the low/mid levels. Additionally, if the \nsubtropical ridge becomes more dominant, then we will have to \nworry about too strong of a subsidence inversion. Given the fairly\nhigh model uncertainty, the forecast is likely to change.\n\nThe temperature forecast is also of lower confidence with a fairly\nwide spread starting Monday. At the moment, NBM forecast\ntemperatures call for readings more in the normal range by early\nnext week, but given the higher model spread we could end up\nwith readings slightly below or even slightly above normal.\nGuidance at least mostly agrees on the 'coolest' temperatures\nfalling on next Tuesday and Wednesday before trending upward\nagain during the latter half of next week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0525Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \n\nThe only aviation weather issue will be low confidence on winds \nFriday evening, with the possibility of distant TS outflows\ncausing sudden gusty winds out of the SE or NE. Winds will \ncontinue to favor SW tonight through around 10-11Z, with a period \nof gusts into the upper teens possible. A somewhat typical, if \ndelayed a bit, southeast wind switch is expected before sunrise \nfollowed by a switch back to SW/W between 17-19Z. Occasional \nafternoon gusts to around 15-18 kts are likely. Chances for \nTSRA/SHRA are too low (10% or less) to include in the TAF Friday \nlate afternoon/evening, and the associated impacts on winds are \nquite uncertain this far out and dependent on strong outflows \nprogressing through the Phoenix airspace. However, if an outflow \ndoes reach the terminals, it will likely emanate from an easterly\ndirection (SE to NE). FEW-SCT mid and high cloud decks will \npersist through the period.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nWinds will continue to oscillate between SE and SW at the\nterminals with speeds 8 to 15 kts sustained (periods with lower \nspeeds likely at KIPL). A few periods with gusts between 20-25 \nkts are possible at KBLH, one Friday afternoon and another in the\nevening after a lull. The possibility of another distant TS\noutflow reaching KIPL cannot be ruled out late Friday\nafternoon/early evening. Periods with FEW-SCT and occasionally BKN\nmid and high cloud decks will continue. \n\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nAbove normal temperatures will continue into the weekend with\nmodestly elevated humidities. Rain chances will improve by \nFriday, but still mostly favor the Arizona high terrain. \nAfternoon minimum humidities of 17-25% today will improve to\n25-30% for Friday and Saturday. Overnight recoveries will remain\nfair to good at 40-70%. Expect fairly typical diurnal winds with \nlittle if any breeziness today, but with 20-25 mph afternoon and\nearly evening gusts on Friday and Saturday, mostly in the western\nportion of the CWA. Chances for isolated showers and \nthunderstorms are likely to remain possible across the eastern \ndistricts through at least early next week while temperatures \neventually fall back into a normal range.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530-532-533.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ562-566-567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan/Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Whittock\nFIRE WEATHER...Ryan/Kuhlman\n",
